<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hacienda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A hacienda was a large landed estate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>, typically producing livestock or crops and relying on dependent or indebted labour. Haciendas became dominant economic and social units in the colonial countryside. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> they were a central feature of colonial society and the rural economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hacienda.jpg" alt="Hacienda" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Eloquent Peasant · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hacienda_Lealtad,_former_coffee_plantation_in_Lares,_Puerto_Rico_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Haciendas originated during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, when the sudden acquisition of conquered land allowed kings to grant large holdings to nobles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and religious military orders as a reward for service. Andalusian haciendas produced wine, grain, oil and livestock and were more purely agricultural than what followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>.</p><p>The word comes from Spanish hacer, to make, and refers to productive enterprises. The term is imprecise, but it usually means landed estates of significant size, while smaller holdings were called estancias or ranchos. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> the model was exported with colonisation, and the crown gave conquistadores large land grants that became haciendas. The system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> is considered to have started in 1529, when the crown granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> the title of Marquis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Haciendas were profit-making enterprises linked to regional or international markets. They grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, wheat, fruit and vegetables and produced meat, wool, leather and tallow. Land distribution ran parallel to the allotment of indigenous people to servitude under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a>. As the crown moved to end encomienda labour, Spaniards consolidated private landholdings and recruited labour on a permanent or casual basis. The owner, called the hacendado or patrón, often lived in a city, while a paid administrator managed the estate. Near indigenous communities, as in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, a small permanent workforce was supplemented at planting and harvest.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a>, horses, sheep and goats came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and sheep in particular did great environmental damage by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/overgrazing">overgrazing</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> haciendas were mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations worked by enslaved people brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> that system ended with abolition on 22 March 1873. The Church, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, held vast hacienda lands or lent money to hacendados.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After independence in the early 19th century the hacienda remained in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> haciendas were prevalent until the Agrarian Reform that followed the 1952 Revolution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-paz-estenssoro">Víctor Paz Estenssoro</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> they lasted until the Agrarian Reform of 1969 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-velasco-alvarado">Juan Velasco Alvarado</a>, who expropriated the land and redistributed it to peasants. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> the institution was eliminated by the land reform of 1962 to 1973. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> it survived efforts at land reform, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> the last haciendas had ended by the 1990s. Today the word also names an architectural style in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> it names the finance ministry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hacienda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mexico entered the late nineteenth century as a largely rural country in which <b>haciendas</b>, Indigenous villages and mining districts were the main economic units.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Large <b>haciendas</b> raised cattle, wheat and maize, and sugar estates relied partly on enslaved Africans, perhaps 200,000 of whom were brought to New Spain, mostly before 1650.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Liberal laws from the 1860s to the 1880s, beginning under President Mariano Melgarejo, attacked communal landholding, and <b>haciendas</b> spread across the highlands at the expense of the ayllus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Nineteenth-century Chile was a society of great landed estates, the <b>haciendas</b> of the Central Valley, where a small creole elite ruled over tenant labourers known as inquilinos, while mining in the arid north provided most export earnings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Sierra, great <b>haciendas</b> bound indigenous labourers through debt servitude known as concertaje and through the huasipungo system, in which families worked the estate in return for a small plot of their own; the special tribute levied on indigenous people was abolished only in 1857, and the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Indigo was El Salvador’s main export in the colonial period and for decades after independence, grown on <b>haciendas</b> and by peasants and sold largely through Guatemalan merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
